WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.834 --> 00:00:07.400
♪♪

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.567
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:10.734
is an Academy Award-winning
actress

00:00:10.734 --> 00:00:13.033
you know from films like
"The Favorite," "Black Widow,"

00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:14.567
and "The Constant Gardener."

00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:17.200
Her new miniseries,
"Dead Ringers,"

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.000
is now streaming
on Prime Video.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.100
Let's take a look.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.100
-What is this?
-Hello?

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.534
Hello?
-What are you doing?

00:00:23.534 --> 00:00:24.868
-Trying to get us the money,
Elliot.

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:27.601
-These are pie charts
and bar graphs.

00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:28.868
Did you make these yourself?

00:00:28.868 --> 00:00:31.200
-Hello, Mantles?
Feel like I'm losing you.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.934
-How exactly are you planning
on using this, you nerd?

00:00:32.934 --> 00:00:34.067
-Mantles?
-Elliot, switch.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.167
-Oh, my God.

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:36.434
You're still just that
14-year-old

00:00:36.434 --> 00:00:38.834
wanking into her
algebra homework.

00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:39.934
-Um...

00:00:39.934 --> 00:00:42.067
-That didn't happen.
-Basically it did.

00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:44.801
-But it didn't.
-But it could have.

00:00:44.801 --> 00:00:45.801
-Elliot!

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.234
-Please welcome to the show,
Rachel Weisz, everybody.

00:01:06.701 --> 00:01:08.267
I'm so happy to
have you here.

00:01:08.267 --> 00:01:10.100
-Thank you for having me.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.400
-Congratulations on this show.
It's based, loosely based --

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.801
it's sort of, I guess,
a new take

00:01:14.801 --> 00:01:18.400
on this David Cronenberg film
from 1988, "Dead Ringers."

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.167
Do you remember the first time
you saw that film?

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:22.868
-I do. I don't remember, like,
where I was exactly,

00:01:22.868 --> 00:01:24.968
but I remember
how it made me feel.

00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:26.000
-It's very intense.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.901
-Very intense, very haunting,
twisted.

00:01:27.901 --> 00:01:29.601
It's iconic.
It's like an incredible movie.

00:01:29.601 --> 00:01:31.901
-But I'm very glad
with the clip you brought

00:01:31.901 --> 00:01:34.300
because this show
is both of those things.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.167
It's twisted, it's haunted,
but it's also very funny,

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.100
which I am glad that we could
show with the clip,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:40.667
because I think
when I heard "Dead Ringers"

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:41.901
was going to be
a miniseries, like,

00:01:41.901 --> 00:01:43.334
"Oh, I don't know
if I could handle it,"

00:01:43.334 --> 00:01:45.367
but it's really -- there's
a lot of humor in this, as well.

00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:47.868
Was that very important to you
when you started making this?

00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:49.200
-Yes. Yes, it was,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:51.634
because I think six hours
of that level of intensity

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:54.968
is a lot to ask of someone
at home watching the TV.

00:01:54.968 --> 00:01:56.801
Right? Don't you think?
It's a lot. It's a lot.

00:01:56.801 --> 00:01:58.100
-You also -- I have to say,

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.467
you have great chemistry
with yourself.

00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:05.000
-That's so weird
to put it like that.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.400
-There's a lot.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.901
-First of all, I'm very,
just on a technical level,

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:11.701
I'm very impressed
with how well the scenes

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.534
where you're acting
against yourself, there is a --

00:02:13.534 --> 00:02:16.267
a because you're playing twins,
there's that very, like,

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:18.367
I don't know,
a sibling-type crosstalk

00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:19.968
that I would think
would be very hard to do,

00:02:19.968 --> 00:02:23.767
considering only one of you
was there at any given time.

00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:28.834
-Yeah, well, that's really
distinct characters.

00:02:28.834 --> 00:02:30.868
And I had
an acting-scene partner

00:02:30.868 --> 00:02:32.267
called Kitty, Kitty Hawthorne,

00:02:32.267 --> 00:02:34.667
who was brilliant,
so I wasn't talking to the air.

00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:36.367
I had someone to talk to.
-That's very nice.

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:38.334
-Yeah, it was really lovely.
She's brilliant.

00:02:38.334 --> 00:02:42.667
And yeah, I mean, they're just
really different people.

00:02:42.667 --> 00:02:44.634
So, when you say
chemistry with yourself,

00:02:44.634 --> 00:02:47.067
that sounds kind of creepy
and weird.

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:50.033
I mean, I kind of dig it
in a way, but for the show,

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:51.434
for the show,
it was like Elliot's, like --

00:02:51.434 --> 00:02:55.100
she's she's very kind
of hungry and slightly...

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.567
She's always
kind of moving around,

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:59.300
and she can't eat enough burgers
and sleep with enough guys.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.767
And she's -- and her appetite
for her career is massive.

00:03:01.767 --> 00:03:04.767
And Beverly's very kind of shy

00:03:04.767 --> 00:03:08.067
and still and thoughtful
and careful.

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:09.634
So, they're just very different.

00:03:09.634 --> 00:03:12.100
-It's very easy to yeah,
when you're watching the show,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.968
it's very easy to tell
the two characters apart,

00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:15.467
even though they're played
by the --

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:17.934
and you don't have any sort of
like big soap-opera visual cues,

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:19.400
like the eye patch
or anything.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.501
-No, no.
-To know which twin is which.

00:03:20.501 --> 00:03:21.501
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:22.534
-So I'm very impressed
with that.

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:24.634
-Eye patch, parrot, cane.
Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:26.934
-Did you have to do --
was it tricky for you?

00:03:26.934 --> 00:03:28.701
Was it simple for you
to keep it straight

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:30.701
because the characters
were so divergent?

00:03:30.701 --> 00:03:31.901
-Yes. Yes.

00:03:31.901 --> 00:03:35.367
They were just
two radically different gals.

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:39.234
Yeah. We learned
to start with Elliot

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:42.067
because she sets the pace,
and she moves around a lot.

00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:44.400
And then Beverly would slot in
with her because she's --

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.000
she was born two minutes later.
So she's the little sister.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.901
-Yeah. There you go.
It doesn't matter. Birth order.

00:03:48.901 --> 00:03:50.934
It doesn't matter how much time.
Birth order matters.

00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:53.400
-It matters. Yeah.
-I did not realize.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:55.968
So, obviously, I was aware
of the Cronenberg film.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:57.701
I didn't realize
the Cronenberg film

00:03:57.701 --> 00:04:00.200
was loosely based
on real-life twins

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.968
who were, you know, OB-GYNs
in New York in the '70s.

00:04:03.968 --> 00:04:06.267
-Yes.
-Were you aware of that?

00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:09.767
-Yes. There was a big
"New York" magazine article,

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:12.634
which I read about them.

00:04:12.634 --> 00:04:15.567
Yeah. They were both found dead
in their apartment.

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:18.067
They, I think -- well,
one of them OD'd.

00:04:18.067 --> 00:04:20.100
But they were
the celebrity doctors

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.167
to get your baby delivered by
if you were a woman

00:04:22.167 --> 00:04:24.601
with a certain amount
of wealth in 1973.

00:04:24.601 --> 00:04:27.501
You wanted these guys
to deliver your babies.

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:29.100
And funnily enough,

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:31.267
the apartment block
where we filmed in Manhattan

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:35.501
that was the lobby of the twins'
home in the TV show,

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:37.300
the woman who managed
the building came up to me

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.033
and she said,

00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:43.267
"I was delivered
by one of those doctors."

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:44.501
-Wow.

00:04:44.501 --> 00:04:46.701
-Which was kind of uncanny
and strange, anyway,

00:04:46.701 --> 00:04:47.734
that we should cross paths.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:48.834
But yeah.

00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:51.667
-What a trip.
-I know, right? I know.

00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:53.834
-I heard for preparation,
you know,

00:04:53.834 --> 00:04:56.234
for playing these characters
who had this professional skill,

00:04:56.234 --> 00:05:00.667
you actually watched
some childbirths happen?

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:02.300
-Yes.
-Obviously you got permission.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:04.000
-I did. I did.
-Okay.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.133
-I did.
-That's very good.

00:05:05.133 --> 00:05:08.167
On an H.R. level, I had to ask.
-Yeah.

00:05:08.167 --> 00:05:10.701
Imagine just like hiding behind
a potted plant, you know.

00:05:10.701 --> 00:05:11.968
-And the doctor being like,

00:05:11.968 --> 00:05:13.634
"We're going to
put you under some anesthesia.

00:05:13.634 --> 00:05:15.501
"You might see
an Academy Award winner.

00:05:15.501 --> 00:05:20.501
That's totally normal.
That's not a real thing."

00:05:20.501 --> 00:05:23.801
I mean, obviously you've
been on the other side of it.

00:05:23.801 --> 00:05:25.834
What did you feel like
watching it?

00:05:25.834 --> 00:05:28.901
I mean, I will say because one
of the things this show does,

00:05:28.901 --> 00:05:32.501
because we see almost everything
on television, it's so rare

00:05:32.501 --> 00:05:35.400
that we see a very realistic
depiction of childbirth,

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.434
which I will say as someone
who went into watching --

00:05:38.434 --> 00:05:40.801
you know, I've seen
all three of my kids get born

00:05:40.801 --> 00:05:43.634
in all very different locations,
I should note.

00:05:43.634 --> 00:05:45.467
Hospital, lobby, and bathtub.

00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:50.467
So, I've seen them all,
but I wish I'd seen more of it

00:05:50.467 --> 00:05:51.934
before I saw it
for the first time

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:55.167
because watching it is -- you
just watch it and you're like,

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:58.734
"Well, this is obviously
going wrong."

00:05:58.734 --> 00:06:02.133
-Yes.
Yeah, It looks scary, right?

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:03.334
-I mean, so, I mean,

00:06:03.334 --> 00:06:05.801
having -- you obviously
had gone through it.

00:06:05.801 --> 00:06:07.167
Was watching it --
were you like,

00:06:07.167 --> 00:06:11.300
"Oh, is that
what it looked like?"

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.601
-It's a really good question.

00:06:13.601 --> 00:06:18.000
Yeah, of course, when you're
giving birth yourself, it's...

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.133
Yeah, you don't know.
You're sort of...

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:23.100
Yeah, it's very intense.
It's very intense.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.234
You can't see it.
Yeah. So, you'd witnessed it...

00:06:26.234 --> 00:06:27.501
-Yeah.
...from the outside.

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:28.767
-Yeah, that would be a weird
case where I'd go, like,

00:06:28.767 --> 00:06:32.801
"As a man,
let me tell you what..."

00:06:32.801 --> 00:06:37.968
-Yeah, it was exhilarating,
actually.

00:06:37.968 --> 00:06:42.300
I shadowed some obstetricians,
and that was the technical term.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:44.667
I wasn't behind them
looking creepy like, but...

00:06:44.667 --> 00:06:47.601
But I shadowed these
obstetricians and it was,

00:06:47.601 --> 00:06:49.734
I suppose
the simplest way to say it is

00:06:49.734 --> 00:06:53.133
that I know that it happens
every second on planet Earth.

00:06:53.133 --> 00:06:54.868
Someone's being born.

00:06:54.868 --> 00:06:58.334
But to actually witness it,
it was exhilarating.

00:06:58.334 --> 00:07:00.400
I had an adrenaline rush
the likes

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:03.334
of which I'd never had,
and it seemed like a miracle.

00:07:03.334 --> 00:07:05.067
It seemed like
this miraculous thing.

00:07:05.067 --> 00:07:08.400
There's a human being
coming out of another.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.133
It's extraordinary.
Absolutely.

00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:12.100
Is that how you felt when...
-I did.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:16.133
You know, you also think
the next one you'll be,

00:07:16.133 --> 00:07:17.167
you know...
-Used to it.

00:07:17.167 --> 00:07:19.868
-Right.
And you aren't at all.

00:07:19.868 --> 00:07:21.834
I mean, it's held --
I do feel like my wife

00:07:21.834 --> 00:07:23.501
has done a nice job
of heightening each time.

00:07:23.501 --> 00:07:24.767
So, I do appreciate it.

00:07:24.767 --> 00:07:26.767
-You mean like the dangerous
places where she --

00:07:26.767 --> 00:07:28.133
-Yeah, I mean, she's, like,

00:07:28.133 --> 00:07:31.300
kind of a thrill mom, you know?
-Yeah, I love that.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:32.601
It's like you've done
the lobby.

00:07:32.601 --> 00:07:33.634
What's next?

00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:34.767
-What's next?
-Yeah, I love that.

00:07:34.767 --> 00:07:36.100
-But yeah, you kind of...

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:37.701
And it's just
that thing of like,

00:07:37.701 --> 00:07:41.000
there wasn't -- like a minute
ago there wasn't a human being.

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:44.033
And now it's here.
And then it dawns on you.

00:07:44.033 --> 00:07:46.200
And you've got to take care
of it forever.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:47.300
-The rest of your life.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.000
-The rest of your life.
-Yeah.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.434
-But, you know,
it must have been...

00:07:51.434 --> 00:07:54.067
I know you're a producer
on this, as well,

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:57.567
so I have to imagine there were
a great amount of conversations

00:07:57.567 --> 00:07:59.934
about the care you took
with trying to depict this

00:07:59.934 --> 00:08:03.000
in a way that was,
you know, I don't know.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.534
I mean, obviously,
it's entertainment,

00:08:04.534 --> 00:08:06.901
but it is like helpful
to put this on screen,

00:08:06.901 --> 00:08:08.834
I think, in a way
that it hasn't been before.

00:08:08.834 --> 00:08:11.367
-Yeah.
I mean, I think Alice Birch,

00:08:11.367 --> 00:08:14.467
the showrunner and writer
extraordinaire of the piece,

00:08:14.467 --> 00:08:17.234
she and I just felt like
it was really interesting

00:08:17.234 --> 00:08:19.200
to show childbirth.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:24.634
We're so used to seeing
violence on the screen.

00:08:24.634 --> 00:08:27.400
Like, there's such
a cinematic language now

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.334
for the ways in which
you can die --

00:08:29.334 --> 00:08:31.567
operatically comedically,
tragic, you know,

00:08:31.567 --> 00:08:33.834
blood spurting, you know,
we're just so used to it.

00:08:33.834 --> 00:08:35.200
But there's something about,
yeah,

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.234
childbirth that seemed like,
yeah, there is some blood,

00:08:38.234 --> 00:08:41.067
but it's beautiful
and interesting.

00:08:41.067 --> 00:08:42.133
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:42.133 --> 00:08:44.334
-And it's, you know,

00:08:44.334 --> 00:08:46.367
it's the kind
that you actually one day

00:08:46.367 --> 00:08:48.367
will be hopefully lucky
to see, right?

00:08:48.367 --> 00:08:52.534
Like, it's the good kind. So.
-Yeah.

00:08:52.534 --> 00:08:54.100
-I know that --
-What do you mean?

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.734
-Well...
-Sorry.

00:08:57.734 --> 00:08:59.200
-You're not allowed
to ask follow-ups of me.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:02.067
-Okay. Sorry. Okay.
-No, what I mean, I guess,

00:09:02.067 --> 00:09:06.367
is when you watch,
like, a gunshot violence

00:09:06.367 --> 00:09:08.934
on a TV show,
like, you hope in real life,

00:09:08.934 --> 00:09:10.634
you hope you'll never
encounter that.

00:09:10.634 --> 00:09:13.000
But when you see
the sort of, you know,

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.934
I don't want to say there's
the violence of childbirth,

00:09:14.934 --> 00:09:15.934
but it does like feel like --

00:09:15.934 --> 00:09:16.934
-It's intense.
-It's intense.

00:09:16.934 --> 00:09:18.267
-It's a lot. It's a lot.

00:09:18.267 --> 00:09:20.634
-So, it's actually an intense
thing that being able

00:09:20.634 --> 00:09:22.501
to see realistically depicted
on a television show

00:09:22.501 --> 00:09:25.667
is like a helpful thing
to see once before you see it.

00:09:25.667 --> 00:09:28.267
-I think
that's really beautifully put.

00:09:28.267 --> 00:09:31.501
-Well, it took some time.

00:09:31.501 --> 00:09:32.868
We got there.
-Yeah.

00:09:32.868 --> 00:09:34.834
-Much like childbirth,
we got there.

00:09:34.834 --> 00:09:36.167
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:09:36.167 --> 00:09:37.567
Well, it's high stakes.
-It's high stakes.

00:09:37.567 --> 00:09:38.801
-It's the highest stakes,

00:09:38.801 --> 00:09:41.601
most dramatic moment
in a human being's life.

00:09:41.601 --> 00:09:44.267
And for the mom,
the dad, the baby.

00:09:44.267 --> 00:09:46.267
-The crazy thing is how, I mean,

00:09:46.267 --> 00:09:48.667
it's high stakes
for the obstetricians,

00:09:48.667 --> 00:09:50.701
but they also have to --
the fact

00:09:50.701 --> 00:09:53.434
that they do it multiple times
in the same day.

00:09:53.434 --> 00:09:55.567
-Yeah, it's just...
-It just blows my mind.

00:09:55.567 --> 00:09:57.000
-Yeah.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.100
For them, I guess it's ordinary
at this point.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:00.467
-You have to make it ordinary,

00:10:00.467 --> 00:10:02.334
like, you don't want one of them
being like, "Oh, no!"

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:05.200
-"Whoa!"
-"Oh, this is bad!"

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.734
-We'd make
a good obstetrician pair.

00:10:07.734 --> 00:10:08.968
Like, "Whoa!"
-"Oh!"

00:10:08.968 --> 00:10:12.300
-"Whoo!"

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.968
-One of the other things,
the last thing I'll say,

00:10:13.968 --> 00:10:15.667
it must have been nice that,

00:10:15.667 --> 00:10:17.868
you know, you make movies
all over the world.

00:10:17.868 --> 00:10:20.133
You actually got to work
on the show in your hometown.

00:10:20.133 --> 00:10:21.701
Was that the best?

00:10:21.701 --> 00:10:22.934
-It was the best.

00:10:22.934 --> 00:10:26.834
I was at Silvercup Studios,
Long Island City,

00:10:26.834 --> 00:10:28.567
and it was just -- yeah,
it was so great

00:10:28.567 --> 00:10:31.334
to be in New York
and all the crew,

00:10:31.334 --> 00:10:34.167
all the technicians
are just so fantastic here.

00:10:34.167 --> 00:10:36.133
And I loved it. I loved it.

00:10:36.133 --> 00:10:38.834
-It's a really wonderful show.
It's so beautifully directed.

00:10:38.834 --> 00:10:41.167
The art design on this show
is just gorgeous

00:10:41.167 --> 00:10:42.634
and you're really
wonderful in it.

00:10:42.634 --> 00:10:44.167
And thanks so much
for being here to talk about it.

00:10:44.167 --> 00:10:45.868
-Thank you, Seth.
Thank you.

00:10:45.868 --> 00:10:47.601
-Rachel Weisz, everybody.

00:10:47.601 --> 00:10:49.000
All six episodes
of "Dead Ringers"

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:50.400
are available now
on Prime Video.

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.033
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

00:10:53.033 --> 00:10:55.834
